WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.328 --> 00:00:04.180
- I remember coming home
on night after a big fight

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:07.033
and I run up to the kitchen and I get ice

00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:08.866
and my hand's swollen.

00:00:10.069 --> 00:00:11.300
Just another day at work.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.368
There was a time in my life
when I said if I don't,

00:00:14.368 --> 00:00:16.410
if I'm not a star by the time I'm 18

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.197
I'm going to have to
get out of the business.

00:00:18.197 --> 00:00:20.292
I remember the studio exec looking at me

00:00:20.292 --> 00:00:23.084
and his food almost
coming out of his mouth

00:00:23.084 --> 00:00:25.087
and going "Vinnie, we're not even sure

00:00:25.087 --> 00:00:27.391
we want to make this one,

00:00:27.391 --> 00:00:30.052
how could you talk about three movies".

00:00:30.052 --> 00:00:32.531
- He's an American
actor, producer, director

00:00:32.531 --> 00:00:33.781
and screenwriter.

00:00:33.781 --> 00:00:35.834
He's best known for his role as Dominic

00:00:35.834 --> 00:00:37.744
in the Fast and the Furious series.

00:00:37.744 --> 00:00:39.927
He made his stage debut at age seven

00:00:39.927 --> 00:00:43.454
in the Dinosaur Door Play and
has been acting every since.

00:00:43.454 --> 00:00:45.106
He's Vin Diesel and here's my take

00:00:45.106 --> 00:00:46.966
on his top ten rules of success.

00:00:46.966 --> 00:00:48.611
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:48.611 --> 00:00:49.485
and make sure to stick around

00:00:49.485 --> 00:00:51.711
all the way til the end
for special bonus clips.

00:00:51.711 --> 00:00:53.174
And as always, if Vin says something

00:00:53.174 --> 00:00:54.982
that resinates with you, please leave it

00:00:54.982 --> 00:00:56.698
in the comments below
and put quotes around it

00:00:56.698 --> 00:00:59.106
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:59.106 --> 00:01:00.349
Enjoy.

00:01:00.349 --> 00:01:02.978
(Swishing and loud beat)

00:01:02.978 --> 00:01:05.561
(upbeat music)

00:01:13.127 --> 00:01:15.629
- I, as you know, was a
bouncer for many years.

00:01:15.629 --> 00:01:19.469
I remember coming home one
night after a big fight

00:01:19.469 --> 00:01:22.332
and I run up to the kitchen, I get ice

00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:26.499
and my hand's swollen and
just another day at work.

00:01:29.606 --> 00:01:32.523
And I was feeling like, just bummed

00:01:33.415 --> 00:01:35.711
about the fact that I was still doing

00:01:35.711 --> 00:01:38.128
this job after all this time.

00:01:41.641 --> 00:01:44.578
At that one moment I was like,

00:01:44.578 --> 00:01:45.911
why are people--

00:01:47.325 --> 00:01:48.682
why do people have so much anger?

00:01:48.682 --> 00:01:50.467
Why do I have to, you know,

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:54.220
why is this my daily life,
to suppress everybody's anger

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.800
or check everybody's anger that comes into

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.550
the giant 3,000 person nightclub.

00:02:00.518 --> 00:02:02.448
And my father, it was so simple,

00:02:02.448 --> 00:02:05.198
but my father said "you know Vin,

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:08.126
I've traveled around the world.

00:02:08.126 --> 00:02:12.293
I've been to India, I've been
to Russia, I've been to China.

00:02:13.622 --> 00:02:16.539
I've been all through South America

00:02:17.431 --> 00:02:19.893
and I've realized something".

00:02:19.893 --> 00:02:21.832
I said "what".

00:02:21.832 --> 00:02:25.832
He said "people, by and
large, are good people".

00:02:33.343 --> 00:02:37.260
Sounded pretty simple
and yet it meant so much.

00:02:39.176 --> 00:02:41.662
And if you look at my Facebook page,

00:02:41.662 --> 00:02:45.702
or if you look at the way
that all that positive energy,

00:02:45.702 --> 00:02:49.055
I don't think there's a page in the world

00:02:49.055 --> 00:02:52.972
in history that harbors
as much positive energy

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:58.258
as one would see on this Facebook page.

00:02:59.253 --> 00:03:00.420
- It's what you put out it.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:01.803
- And it's really that.
- Right.

00:03:01.803 --> 00:03:05.470
- And I say that because,
I say that because

00:03:06.851 --> 00:03:09.601
if you believe that in your heart

00:03:10.581 --> 00:03:13.413
and not everybody
watching this can say that

00:03:13.413 --> 00:03:15.658
or is ready to say that or is comfortable

00:03:15.658 --> 00:03:17.894
with themselves enough to say that,

00:03:17.894 --> 00:03:21.265
but if you really believe in your heart

00:03:21.265 --> 00:03:24.765
that people, by and large, are good people

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:30.065
it affects the way that
you address the world.

00:03:30.065 --> 00:03:32.815
As you see, post in and post out,

00:03:34.907 --> 00:03:37.657
I address a world that in my soul

00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:42.050
I feel is comprised of good people.

00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:47.254
My advice to people who
want to pursue their dream

00:03:47.254 --> 00:03:51.337
is to find any way that
they can to make product.

00:03:54.931 --> 00:03:58.762
My philosophy is that product
is the most important thing.

00:03:58.762 --> 00:04:01.566
I started acting when
I was seven years old

00:04:01.566 --> 00:04:04.275
and I didn't get paid as an actor

00:04:04.275 --> 00:04:07.275
until I did Saving Private Ryan

00:04:07.275 --> 00:04:09.945
and I was 30 at that time.

00:04:09.945 --> 00:04:11.591
So, there were many, many years

00:04:11.591 --> 00:04:13.841
of unemployment, obviously.

00:04:15.228 --> 00:04:19.889
What I learned later was
that I had to have something.

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:22.081
I had to have product regardless

00:04:22.081 --> 00:04:24.309
of how big or small it was

00:04:24.309 --> 00:04:25.427
and the first thing that I did

00:04:25.427 --> 00:04:28.353
was direct a short film
called Multi-Facial.

00:04:28.353 --> 00:04:31.103
(dramatic music)

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.943
(exploding and falling rocks)

00:04:44.017 --> 00:04:47.100
(music intensifying)

00:04:50.973 --> 00:04:53.223
(cracking)

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:58.845
(dramatic music)

00:05:21.409 --> 00:05:24.659
(swooshing as falling)

00:05:27.892 --> 00:05:30.642
(dramatic music)

00:05:54.711 --> 00:05:57.628
- No, Groot, you can't, you'll die.

00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:01.972
Why are you doing this, why?

00:06:09.809 --> 00:06:11.059
- We are Groot.

00:06:15.629 --> 00:06:18.099
(dramatic music)

00:06:18.099 --> 00:06:20.526
- So, Vin, voice of Groot and possibly

00:06:20.526 --> 00:06:23.036
my favorite character in the film.

00:06:23.036 --> 00:06:25.338
What were the difficulties of

00:06:25.338 --> 00:06:28.774
kind of expressing yourself
with such limited vocabulary?

00:06:28.774 --> 00:06:30.928
- Clearly the challenge

00:06:30.928 --> 00:06:34.178
and clearly a challenge that a thespian

00:06:36.211 --> 00:06:40.294
would never expect to be confronted with.

00:06:40.294 --> 00:06:44.461
And yet, thank God that I
did have the opportunity

00:06:46.007 --> 00:06:47.418
and the challenge to do it

00:06:47.418 --> 00:06:50.668
because he is such a special character.

00:06:52.376 --> 00:06:54.836
Yes, when Kevin Feige called me

00:06:54.836 --> 00:06:58.003
he said "let me prefec this phone call

00:07:00.432 --> 00:07:03.512
by telling that the character
we're about to discuss

00:07:03.512 --> 00:07:05.320
is the strangest character Marvel

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:08.884
has ever attempted to
put on the big screen".

00:07:08.884 --> 00:07:13.427
And then he said "however,
because Iron Giant

00:07:13.427 --> 00:07:16.010
is a family favorite we believe

00:07:17.621 --> 00:07:20.288
that you can bring life to this,

00:07:21.493 --> 00:07:25.326
even though most people
will only hear you say

00:07:26.256 --> 00:07:27.256
I am Groot".

00:07:31.506 --> 00:07:34.340
And it was a shocker and
something very challenging

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:36.171
and something very interesting.

00:07:36.171 --> 00:07:38.742
It was really my son,
my three year old son,

00:07:38.742 --> 00:07:40.640
when I came home with the
book of conceptual art

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:43.769
and showed him all the
characters in the Galaxy,

00:07:43.769 --> 00:07:46.136
he pointed to the tree
(laughing)

00:07:46.136 --> 00:07:49.052
as a character that he thought was daddy.

00:07:49.052 --> 00:07:51.013
- Everyone loves the tree,
everyone loves the tree.

00:07:51.013 --> 00:07:51.888
(laughing)

00:07:51.888 --> 00:07:54.517
- Sometimes you see someone
having enormous success

00:07:54.517 --> 00:07:56.894
and there's just something about them,

00:07:56.894 --> 00:08:00.965
you say good for him
and I think not just me,

00:08:00.965 --> 00:08:03.275
but when I read about the response to you

00:08:03.275 --> 00:08:04.697
lots of people are saying that.

00:08:04.697 --> 00:08:08.864
Cheering for you to have the
accomplishments you have had.

00:08:09.805 --> 00:08:10.972
- I feel that.

00:08:12.101 --> 00:08:15.268
I've always felt the sense of support.

00:08:16.774 --> 00:08:19.107
And I think that my struggle

00:08:20.037 --> 00:08:23.954
to achieving this goal
and achieving this dream

00:08:26.542 --> 00:08:29.292
is something that speaks volumes.

00:08:31.056 --> 00:08:34.918
I started out acting at seven years old

00:08:34.918 --> 00:08:37.527
and I struggled for so long.

00:08:37.527 --> 00:08:40.708
There was a time in my life
when I said if I don't,

00:08:40.708 --> 00:08:42.666
if I'm not a star by the time I'm 18,

00:08:42.666 --> 00:08:44.902
I'm going to have to
get out of the business.

00:08:44.902 --> 00:08:47.219
And then of course it jumped to 21

00:08:47.219 --> 00:08:51.136
and then 25 and then I'm
looking in the mirror,

00:08:53.468 --> 00:08:56.739
just past my mid 20s and I haven't,

00:08:56.739 --> 00:09:00.132
I haven't received
health benefits from SAG.

00:09:00.132 --> 00:09:04.299
And we all know that with
the Screen Actor's Guild

00:09:05.699 --> 00:09:08.482
you got to make $7,000
in order to be eligible

00:09:08.482 --> 00:09:10.421
for health benefits.

00:09:10.421 --> 00:09:12.877
So, in fact, I didn't get health benefits

00:09:12.877 --> 00:09:14.749
until I was 30 years old,

00:09:14.749 --> 00:09:17.347
even while I was shooting
Saving Private Ryan

00:09:17.347 --> 00:09:20.234
I wasn't covered.
(laughing)

00:09:20.234 --> 00:09:23.734
And I think that that kind of rise to fame

00:09:27.366 --> 00:09:30.866
is something that's empowering for people.

00:09:32.122 --> 00:09:35.757
So, when they celebrate that story

00:09:35.757 --> 00:09:38.699
I think that they are also celebrating

00:09:38.699 --> 00:09:42.167
a, you know, a story that's about a guy

00:09:42.167 --> 00:09:44.151
who was proactive and resourceful

00:09:44.151 --> 00:09:48.318
and did pay his dues and
did struggle and persevered.

00:09:49.643 --> 00:09:51.230
- And believed in himself?

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:52.266
- I think you have to.

00:09:52.266 --> 00:09:56.099
I think the only thing
I had for so many years

00:09:57.813 --> 00:10:01.980
was this confidence that
I held onto very tightly.

00:10:04.813 --> 00:10:07.292
- Now, was it a difference
in confidence and arrogance?

00:10:07.292 --> 00:10:08.410
- Oh, absolutely.

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:09.972
- You just knew that you were getting

00:10:09.972 --> 00:10:12.900
the skills necessary to be good.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:16.876
Then when moment came you'd
be ready and could deliver.

00:10:16.876 --> 00:10:17.912
- Right.

00:10:17.912 --> 00:10:20.420
I needed to be confident at

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:23.081
the risk even of being arrogant.

00:10:23.081 --> 00:10:27.075
I needed to be confident
because I had nothing else.

00:10:27.075 --> 00:10:29.278
And if you go into a situation where

00:10:29.278 --> 00:10:32.895
you're dedicating your
whole life to something,

00:10:32.895 --> 00:10:35.588
literally your whole life,
all of your decisions

00:10:35.588 --> 00:10:38.088
are made with one common goal,

00:10:40.483 --> 00:10:42.340
then I think that you'd
better believe in yourself.

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:45.370
Especially if you start to work,

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:47.718
to save money to make films.

00:10:47.718 --> 00:10:50.347
I think you could ask
most people in the world

00:10:50.347 --> 00:10:54.180
and they would tell you
that I'm not Hollywood

00:10:56.231 --> 00:10:57.187
- [Interviewer] I know, I was speechless.

00:10:57.187 --> 00:11:00.337
- I knew, but in a good way.

00:11:00.337 --> 00:11:03.119
And it's not a bad question to ask because

00:11:03.119 --> 00:11:04.482
it's not even so Hollywood.

00:11:04.482 --> 00:11:07.814
How do you stay ground when
you have 90 million people

00:11:07.814 --> 00:11:10.925
waiting for you to say something?

00:11:10.925 --> 00:11:12.937
So, while I'm here right now,

00:11:12.937 --> 00:11:16.350
and this will go out
to however many people,

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:20.373
at home, I know the second I hear "boom"

00:11:20.373 --> 00:11:22.222
and my computer comes on,

00:11:22.222 --> 00:11:26.915
there are 90 million people
waiting to hear something.

00:11:26.915 --> 00:11:28.346
That's tricky.

00:11:28.346 --> 00:11:30.357
How do you make sense of that?

00:11:30.357 --> 00:11:33.364
What would Marlon Brando
have done if he could have,

00:11:33.364 --> 00:11:36.128
would there have been a
sequel to On The Waterfront?

00:11:36.128 --> 00:11:39.866
Maybe, if there was some
fan out in Texas that said

00:11:39.866 --> 00:11:43.699
I can't wait to see
another On The Waterfront.

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:48.581
- [Interviewer] So do you
feel the pressure constantly

00:11:48.581 --> 00:11:50.330
- Family--
- to deliver?

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:52.478
- I do feel pressure.

00:11:52.478 --> 00:11:55.395
Family keeps you grounded because--

00:11:58.761 --> 00:12:02.247
My toughest task is not going to be unlike

00:12:02.247 --> 00:12:05.384
that other guy's toughest task.

00:12:05.384 --> 00:12:10.029
The toughest task is to be
the best parent you can be.

00:12:10.029 --> 00:12:12.426
We all share that, we all--

00:12:12.426 --> 00:12:15.808
to be the best person you can be.

00:12:15.808 --> 00:12:17.798
We're all growing as individuals.

00:12:17.798 --> 00:12:21.305
We're all growing into our better selves.

00:12:21.305 --> 00:12:24.972
Those are the things
that I stay focused on.

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:29.310
And I don't even know
if I'm that grounded.

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:32.194
I live in a fantasy world of scenes

00:12:32.194 --> 00:12:34.062
in my head and future--

00:12:34.062 --> 00:12:36.238
When I came here and I was about

00:12:36.238 --> 00:12:38.401
to do Fast and Furious four,

00:12:38.401 --> 00:12:40.305
they didn't think I was
grounded when I said

00:12:40.305 --> 00:12:43.767
I can shoot three movies at the same time.

00:12:43.767 --> 00:12:47.944
They thought I was insane
and they were like,

00:12:47.944 --> 00:12:50.919
security, get him off the lot.
(laughing)

00:12:50.919 --> 00:12:52.379
They never imagined it.

00:12:52.379 --> 00:12:54.515
I remember the studio exec looking at me

00:12:54.515 --> 00:12:57.270
and his food almost
coming out of his mouth

00:12:57.270 --> 00:12:59.342
and going "Vinnie, we're not even sure

00:12:59.342 --> 00:13:01.644
we want to make this one.

00:13:01.644 --> 00:13:04.685
How could you talk about three movies?

00:13:04.685 --> 00:13:08.018
Who cares what happens in five and six?"

00:13:12.548 --> 00:13:14.731
And now the only thing they want to know

00:13:14.731 --> 00:13:18.316
is what's going to happen after this.

00:13:18.316 --> 00:13:21.040
How surreal.
- Incredible.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:22.481
- It's incredible.

00:13:22.481 --> 00:13:24.771
One of the interesting things
(coughing)

00:13:24.771 --> 00:13:27.354
about the way that I did it was

00:13:29.107 --> 00:13:30.947
or one of the interesting things about

00:13:30.947 --> 00:13:34.828
being paid the amount of money
I'm being paid now for film

00:13:34.828 --> 00:13:38.578
is back in 1996 I was
working to make movies.

00:13:42.191 --> 00:13:44.858
So, whether I was saving $3,000,

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:50.826
putting a savings of $3,000 together,

00:13:50.826 --> 00:13:53.398
which at the time was a feat.
- Yeah

00:13:53.398 --> 00:13:55.795
- Or working the whole year telemarketing

00:13:55.795 --> 00:13:59.128
to save $47,000, I was taking that money

00:14:00.367 --> 00:14:03.383
and investing it in my career

00:14:03.383 --> 00:14:05.425
and investing in something
that I believed in.

00:14:05.425 --> 00:14:06.918
- How do you invest in your career?

00:14:06.918 --> 00:14:08.149
- Well,
- At that stage.

00:14:08.149 --> 00:14:12.871
- At that stage, if people
aren't offering you roles.

00:14:12.871 --> 00:14:14.659
The interesting thing
about being an actor,

00:14:14.659 --> 00:14:18.159
it's different than the other art mediums.

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:24.767
If you're an actor you're
dependent upon on a director,

00:14:24.767 --> 00:14:28.779
a screenwriter, a producer,
a studio to fund it,

00:14:28.779 --> 00:14:30.701
a studio to market the film.

00:14:30.701 --> 00:14:34.236
Even after I made Strays, I didn't have--

00:14:34.236 --> 00:14:36.834
and I went to dramatic
competition at Sundance,

00:14:36.834 --> 00:14:38.543
I didn't have a distributor.

00:14:38.543 --> 00:14:41.112
So, I could only make the film

00:14:41.112 --> 00:14:43.774
and thank God there was a forum.

00:14:43.774 --> 00:14:47.857
Thank God Robert Redford
had put together a forum

00:14:49.215 --> 00:14:51.423
for these films to be shown.

00:14:51.423 --> 00:14:54.553
And even then, even
after you make the film

00:14:54.553 --> 00:14:55.742
and you put the film in the can,

00:14:55.742 --> 00:14:57.349
you still have to have somebody come along

00:14:57.349 --> 00:14:59.787
and spend money to distribute the film.

00:14:59.787 --> 00:15:03.127
So, as an actor, you need all these

00:15:03.127 --> 00:15:06.012
external factors to be artistic.

00:15:06.012 --> 00:15:08.056
If you're a musician you could, you know,

00:15:08.056 --> 00:15:09.885
woodshed and make music.

00:15:09.885 --> 00:15:12.270
If you're a painter
- You could do it alone.

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:13.217
- You could do it alone.

00:15:13.217 --> 00:15:17.036
And being an actor is
a lot less autonomous.

00:15:17.036 --> 00:15:19.453
So, I needed to find a way to

00:15:23.314 --> 00:15:26.147
fulfill all those other positions.

00:15:27.407 --> 00:15:29.825
I had to learn how to write screenplays.

00:15:29.825 --> 00:15:33.107
So I went to college and
learned how to write screenplays

00:15:33.107 --> 00:15:35.493
and I was horrible in
english in high school

00:15:35.493 --> 00:15:39.171
and became an english major in college.

00:15:39.171 --> 00:15:43.088
I started directing and
learning how to direct.

00:15:44.189 --> 00:15:47.287
And learning really
about the film business

00:15:47.287 --> 00:15:51.260
which is probably the number one thing

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:55.427
that separates the unemployed
Vin and the employed Vin.

00:15:56.574 --> 00:15:59.497
It was the understanding of Hollywood

00:15:59.497 --> 00:16:01.177
and the understanding of the business

00:16:01.177 --> 00:16:03.534
and the understanding of strategy.

00:16:03.534 --> 00:16:07.701
Because so many years I was
just concerned with the craft

00:16:10.433 --> 00:16:12.738
and all I thought about was how to make

00:16:12.738 --> 00:16:14.903
the craft as good as possible.

00:16:14.903 --> 00:16:16.813
I've been acting since
I was seven years old

00:16:16.813 --> 00:16:19.922
so that's always been a comfort zone.

00:16:19.922 --> 00:16:23.904
People would often ask me
what do I enjoy doing more,

00:16:23.904 --> 00:16:26.180
acting or directing, 'cause I started

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:28.240
my career as a director.

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:30.393
And I would say directing offers me

00:16:30.393 --> 00:16:33.060
the opportunity to tell a story.

00:16:33.929 --> 00:16:37.762
Acting is more of a
therapeutic process for me

00:16:40.146 --> 00:16:44.676
because as a kid I was
always, like most kids,

00:16:44.676 --> 00:16:49.045
curious about my identity,
in search of my identity.

00:16:49.045 --> 00:16:51.462
And when you play a character

00:16:52.428 --> 00:16:54.874
the parameters of that character

00:16:54.874 --> 00:16:57.081
and that identity are clear.

00:16:57.081 --> 00:16:59.439
There's something therapeutic about that.

00:16:59.439 --> 00:17:03.604
There's no guessing games
when you fall into character.

00:17:03.604 --> 00:17:07.771
So, if I'm not in character
and I'm usually in character,

00:17:08.733 --> 00:17:10.695
unlike other actors that can turn it off

00:17:10.695 --> 00:17:13.438
when the director says cut,

00:17:13.438 --> 00:17:16.599
I've never had that ability.

00:17:16.599 --> 00:17:19.786
I'm usually somewhat in the character

00:17:19.786 --> 00:17:22.183
even after the director says cut.

00:17:22.183 --> 00:17:26.206
- If you could talk to your younger self,

00:17:26.206 --> 00:17:28.508
knowing what you know, in those days

00:17:28.508 --> 00:17:31.739
when you thought maybe
it's not going to happen,

00:17:31.739 --> 00:17:33.163
what would you tell yourself?

00:17:33.163 --> 00:17:35.227
- Play it cool.

00:17:35.227 --> 00:17:37.644
I'd say, I'd say play it cool

00:17:40.977 --> 00:17:43.200
and learn as much as you can

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:46.776
because everything you
learn you're going to need.

00:17:46.776 --> 00:17:49.438
You won't have enough time to learn.

00:17:49.438 --> 00:17:51.582
Everything is an education.

00:17:51.582 --> 00:17:53.521
- I see that the way you use social media,

00:17:53.521 --> 00:17:54.873
the way you reach out to your fans,

00:17:54.873 --> 00:17:57.170
and there is a very direct approach

00:17:57.170 --> 00:17:58.441
that you utilize.
- Yeah.

00:17:58.441 --> 00:17:59.719
- And some people don't do that

00:17:59.719 --> 00:18:01.370
and some people can't do that,

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:03.735
but I think it, for you, really embodies

00:18:03.735 --> 00:18:06.892
who you are as a person
and when you meet people.

00:18:06.892 --> 00:18:07.951
Embracing something like that,

00:18:07.951 --> 00:18:09.273
is that ever scary for you or

00:18:09.273 --> 00:18:10.816
is it just, look, these are the people

00:18:10.816 --> 00:18:12.056
that want to buy your tickets

00:18:12.056 --> 00:18:14.068
and you'd love to talk back to them?

00:18:14.068 --> 00:18:18.061
- I think you have to be, I
think there's something scary

00:18:18.061 --> 00:18:21.696
about talking to a group
of people anywhere.

00:18:21.696 --> 00:18:24.157
If you walk up on stage at Comic-Con,

00:18:24.157 --> 00:18:28.218
you, yourself, you can
feel, we all feel that,

00:18:28.218 --> 00:18:31.256
that little bit of anxiety before

00:18:31.256 --> 00:18:33.673
speaking to a body of people.

00:18:36.752 --> 00:18:39.585
So, it's no different on Facebook.

00:18:40.573 --> 00:18:43.711
So, if you're, when you sit down,

00:18:43.711 --> 00:18:45.479
there are times I could sit down

00:18:45.479 --> 00:18:50.395
to write a post and be at that
chair for two or three hours

00:18:50.395 --> 00:18:53.788
and scrap the whole post
and not put anything up

00:18:53.788 --> 00:18:56.684
because you do want to be thoughtful

00:18:56.684 --> 00:19:00.148
about what you're saying,
even if what you're saying

00:19:00.148 --> 00:19:04.893
is something kind of trite
or seemingly insignificant.

00:19:04.893 --> 00:19:06.893
As everybody on the page knows,

00:19:06.893 --> 00:19:08.151
there's always something cryptic

00:19:08.151 --> 00:19:10.651
behind every damn thing I say.

00:19:12.755 --> 00:19:15.172
And I embraced it differently

00:19:16.738 --> 00:19:18.838
than I think other people have

00:19:18.838 --> 00:19:23.524
or looked at it differently
than other people did early on,

00:19:23.524 --> 00:19:26.258
when, you know, no one had a million fans

00:19:26.258 --> 00:19:30.252
and it was brand new and
Obama had just gotten elected

00:19:30.252 --> 00:19:34.173
and he used the social
media platform to do that

00:19:34.173 --> 00:19:35.923
and it was still new.

00:19:38.593 --> 00:19:40.450
I looked at it as an opportunity

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:43.200
to for the first time in my life,

00:19:44.271 --> 00:19:46.688
be able to hear the audiences

00:19:49.988 --> 00:19:52.571
thought-out reaction to my work

00:19:54.003 --> 00:19:56.103
or whatever film I had done,

00:19:56.103 --> 00:19:59.021
or characters that I might not have done,

00:19:59.021 --> 00:20:00.707
for example Riddick.

00:20:00.707 --> 00:20:05.514
And so, I feel like if I
didn't have that social media,

00:20:05.514 --> 00:20:06.681
I don't think I would have ever

00:20:06.681 --> 00:20:10.318
been pushed to make
Riddick at the same level.

00:20:10.318 --> 00:20:12.568
It was the audience saying,

00:20:13.539 --> 00:20:17.289
I remember this one
comment on a Riddick post

00:20:18.668 --> 00:20:22.835
where, and this was years
before we ever made the movie,

00:20:24.987 --> 00:20:27.863
when it was still kind
of, we were uncertain

00:20:27.863 --> 00:20:30.647
if we'd ever have the
rights to make the movie

00:20:30.647 --> 00:20:33.328
and someone said "hey,
Vin, there really needs

00:20:33.328 --> 00:20:37.331
to be another Riddick
and we're really happy

00:20:37.331 --> 00:20:40.297
that you're doing Fast,
but we need another Riddick

00:20:40.297 --> 00:20:43.964
and I'm sure that if
all of us gave you $10,

00:20:45.742 --> 00:20:47.275
you could surely get the money

00:20:47.275 --> 00:20:51.858
to make it then rated
R, the way you want to".

00:20:51.858 --> 00:20:54.141
And this was before
Kickstarter and all that stuff

00:20:54.141 --> 00:20:57.758
and there was something
so endearing about that

00:20:57.758 --> 00:21:00.479
and that sentiment that
I felt even if I have

00:21:00.479 --> 00:21:02.545
to go make this movie for free,

00:21:02.545 --> 00:21:04.252
I've got to go make this movie.

00:21:04.252 --> 00:21:06.942
It's something that has resinated

00:21:06.942 --> 00:21:11.686
and sometimes the audience,
the film-going audience,

00:21:11.686 --> 00:21:14.936
vetoes the studio and in this situation

00:21:16.430 --> 00:21:18.474
that's exactly what happened.

00:21:18.474 --> 00:21:20.993
The studio initially, the last decade,

00:21:20.993 --> 00:21:23.786
didn't think, didn't know whether they

00:21:23.786 --> 00:21:25.864
were going to be able to revisit this

00:21:25.864 --> 00:21:29.947
at the high level and
still maintain that rated R

00:21:31.129 --> 00:21:32.379
and it might not have been made,

00:21:32.379 --> 00:21:34.371
but it was that audience that said

00:21:34.371 --> 00:21:37.114
it has to be made and that's how we--

00:21:37.114 --> 00:21:40.614
Back in 2002, it was you, the MTV audience

00:21:43.015 --> 00:21:46.245
that first acknowledged this brotherhood

00:21:46.245 --> 00:21:49.324
between Paul Walker and myself

00:21:49.324 --> 00:21:52.824
by honoring us with the Best Team on Film.

00:21:55.928 --> 00:22:00.095
So, it's surreal to be
standing here 12 years later

00:22:02.833 --> 00:22:05.666
and that you have been so generous

00:22:06.531 --> 00:22:08.864
to give us this award again.

00:22:15.969 --> 00:22:18.969
I wish he could be here to see this,

00:22:21.435 --> 00:22:24.018
but I know he's here in spirit.

00:22:26.239 --> 00:22:30.156
Thank you for honoring
me and more importantly,

00:22:33.146 --> 00:22:35.779
thank you for honoring that angel,

00:22:35.779 --> 00:22:37.779
my brother, Paul Walker.

00:22:42.259 --> 00:22:43.389
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:22:43.389 --> 00:22:46.640
I made this video because
C.J. Jones asked me to.

00:22:46.640 --> 00:22:47.746
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur that

00:22:47.746 --> 00:22:50.003
you want me to profile next, leave it

00:22:50.003 --> 00:22:51.658
in the comments below and
I'll see what I can do.

00:22:51.658 --> 00:22:53.060
I'm also curious to know which one

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:54.704
of Vin Diesel's top ten rules had

00:22:54.704 --> 00:22:56.676
the biggest impact on you and why.

00:22:56.676 --> 00:22:58.386
What did you learn from it,
what are you going to apply

00:22:58.386 --> 00:23:00.152
to your life or your business?

00:23:00.152 --> 00:23:02.293
Leave it in the comments, I'm
going to join in the discussion.

00:23:02.293 --> 00:23:04.685
Finally, I want to give a
quick shout out to Sadaf Virji.

00:23:04.685 --> 00:23:07.274
Sadaf, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book

00:23:07.274 --> 00:23:09.351
and taking that picture
and posting it on Facebook.

00:23:09.351 --> 00:23:11.776
It really means a lot to
me and I hope you enjoy it

00:23:11.776 --> 00:23:13.162
and find your one word.

00:23:13.162 --> 00:23:14.544
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:23:14.544 --> 00:23:16.310
I believe in you, I hope you continue

00:23:16.310 --> 00:23:19.951
to believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is.

00:23:19.951 --> 00:23:22.284
Much love, I'll see ya soon.

00:23:28.103 --> 00:23:30.369
- So, Vin, I've heard you're really

00:23:30.369 --> 00:23:34.534
into Dungeons and Dragons.
(audience wooing)

00:23:34.534 --> 00:23:36.704
So, I have two questions.
- Yes.

00:23:36.704 --> 00:23:38.137
- What character do you play

00:23:38.137 --> 00:23:40.428
and would you be willing
to join our group?

00:23:40.428 --> 00:23:42.678
(laughing)

00:23:44.246 --> 00:23:47.845
- You know, it's whenever
anyone asks me to join

00:23:47.845 --> 00:23:51.708
their campaign, there's
a good old feeling I have

00:23:51.708 --> 00:23:54.478
where I feel like, you know,
I'm not alone in the world.

00:23:54.478 --> 00:23:56.511
(laughing)

00:23:56.511 --> 00:23:59.150
And it's okay to be a dweeb.

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:02.650
I played a lot of characters and I played,

00:24:05.553 --> 00:24:08.204
my most famous character
you might have read about,

00:24:08.204 --> 00:24:11.668
which was my witch hunter,
my half-drow witch hunter.

00:24:11.668 --> 00:24:13.630
The irony is I'm about to next month

00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:17.073
do a movie called The Witch Hunter.

00:24:17.073 --> 00:24:19.206
Kind of freaky.
(laughing)

00:24:19.206 --> 00:24:21.839
I mean, I never thought
in a million years,

00:24:21.839 --> 00:24:26.006
when I was rolling the 20-sided
die 20 something years ago

00:24:27.020 --> 00:24:29.100
that Hollywood would make a
movie about a witch hunter,

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.071
but that's another story.

00:24:31.071 --> 00:24:33.977
(laughing)

00:24:33.977 --> 00:24:36.527
Where are you playing
in, on the east coast?

00:24:36.527 --> 00:24:39.016
- Well, we actually do it over video chat.

00:24:39.016 --> 00:24:41.695
- You are doing video chat.
- Yeah.

00:24:41.695 --> 00:24:45.752
So, we've got one guy
down in North Carolina

00:24:45.752 --> 00:24:47.761
and he will video in.

00:24:47.761 --> 00:24:49.613
- Are you using modules, I mean--

00:24:49.613 --> 00:24:50.658
(laughing)

00:24:50.658 --> 00:24:52.173
- It's mostly second edition rules,

00:24:52.173 --> 00:24:56.317
but they have some holdovers
from the first edition, so.

00:24:56.317 --> 00:24:59.618
- Okay, okay.
- He's thinking about it.

00:24:59.618 --> 00:25:02.618
- I mean, we played with everything.

00:25:03.599 --> 00:25:04.432
That's were the witch hunter,

00:25:04.432 --> 00:25:07.257
witch hunter wasn't created
by Gary Gygax, as you know.

00:25:07.257 --> 00:25:08.708
It was the aftermarket books,

00:25:08.708 --> 00:25:09.695
that we were trying to incorporate

00:25:09.695 --> 00:25:11.812
and create new games and trying to create.

00:25:11.812 --> 00:25:12.984
It was a special time.

00:25:12.984 --> 00:25:14.368
(laughing)

00:25:14.368 --> 00:25:16.989
D &amp; D was a special time.

00:25:16.989 --> 00:25:18.980
(laughing)

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:22.016
Yeah, man, we might have to get into that.

00:25:22.016 --> 00:25:24.332
We might have to get into that.

00:25:24.332 --> 00:25:25.441
I could get into that, man.

00:25:25.441 --> 00:25:27.691
(laughing)

00:25:28.841 --> 00:25:30.549
Did you know that Gary Gygax--

00:25:30.549 --> 00:25:32.723
(laughing)
I'm sorry, guys.

00:25:32.723 --> 00:25:35.295
- I told you it was
going to get dorky, yeah.

00:25:35.295 --> 00:25:36.473
- Let's split up into breakout sessions

00:25:36.473 --> 00:25:39.115
where everyone gets their
own Guardian of the Galaxy.

00:25:39.115 --> 00:25:41.927
- Well, I'll tell ya
later, it's Gary Gygax, so.

00:25:41.927 --> 00:25:46.094
The movie opens up tonight
at midnight in America, so.

00:25:49.137 --> 00:25:51.740
- [Interviewer] How do
you explain the success?

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:52.809
All over the world, I mean.

00:25:52.809 --> 00:25:55.308
In Australian and in the U.K.

00:25:55.308 --> 00:25:57.278
- Well, I have a Facebook page.

00:25:57.278 --> 00:25:58.111
- [Interviewer] Yeah, I know.

00:25:58.111 --> 00:26:00.680
- Almost 23 million people.
- Yeah.

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:02.347
- That doesn't hurt.

00:26:04.166 --> 00:26:08.373
But I think the audience
really appreciates

00:26:08.373 --> 00:26:10.743
the integrity that goes into this film.

00:26:10.743 --> 00:26:12.641
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:26:12.641 --> 00:26:16.359
- So many action films of
this size are gratuitous

00:26:16.359 --> 00:26:19.529
and don't take the time to work out story

00:26:19.529 --> 00:26:21.275
and character and add heart,

00:26:21.275 --> 00:26:24.275
and feeling and emotion and romance.

00:26:26.604 --> 00:26:28.712
I think that's what elevates this movie.

00:26:28.712 --> 00:26:32.044
I think this movie, if you didn't see it

00:26:32.044 --> 00:26:33.699
and if you didn't see the last one,

00:26:33.699 --> 00:26:35.619
you could write it off
as just an action movie.

00:26:35.619 --> 00:26:36.452
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:26:36.452 --> 00:26:40.302
- But I think that, the fact
that it's deeper than that,

00:26:40.302 --> 00:26:42.722
and the fact that it's multi-cultural.

00:26:42.722 --> 00:26:43.706
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:26:43.706 --> 00:26:45.506
- No action movie has subtitles

00:26:45.506 --> 00:26:47.901
of people talking in spanish.
- Yeah.

00:26:47.901 --> 00:26:51.854
- But that's, or Portuguese,
but that says something

00:26:51.854 --> 00:26:54.080
about what kind of franchise this is.

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:56.747
(motor running)

00:27:02.471 --> 00:27:04.857
- You got some serious balls, man.

00:27:04.857 --> 00:27:06.274
- I've been told.

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:09.958
- You know, you're lucky I missed my shot.

00:27:09.958 --> 00:27:11.502
- I think you hit your mark.

00:27:11.502 --> 00:27:12.335
- Really?

00:27:13.595 --> 00:27:14.997
What is it with you?

00:27:14.997 --> 00:27:16.751
What'd you got, a death wish or something?

00:27:16.751 --> 00:27:18.846
- If that's what it takes.

00:27:18.846 --> 00:27:20.346
I just want to race.

00:27:21.986 --> 00:27:23.428
- You might lose your car.

00:27:23.428 --> 00:27:25.761
(chuckling)

00:27:27.643 --> 00:27:29.328
- Let's do it.

00:27:29.328 --> 00:27:30.578
- Your funeral.

00:27:31.991 --> 00:27:34.653
- Ride or die, remember?

00:27:34.653 --> 00:27:35.486
(upbeat music)

